From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: the physical aperture's end covers its twig padding

On ARM_LARGE_MEMORY kernels without a page-protection monitor (Apple's
large-memory virtual machines, and the SBSA kernel), arm_vm_init() set
physmap_end = physmap_base + real_phys_size. arm_vm_physmap_init() maps
the memory below the kernel at physmap_base and the memory above it right
after, moved up so its offset within a twig matches its physical one (for
block mappings): up to a twig (32 MB with 16K pages) of padding. When the
kernel's own region, which the aperture skips, is smaller than that
padding, the aperture ends past physmap_end, and copyio's
copy_validate_kernel_addr() panics on the highest pages' aperture
addresses ("kaddr not in kernel"). On QEMU virt with 4 GB, the OpenZFS
test suite reached them through HFS+ writes (cluster_write ->
uio_copyin_phys_user -> bcopy_phys -> copyin) once memory filled
(P3-01 checkpoint 2). The !ARM_LARGE_MEMORY case already allows
PTOV_TABLE_SIZE twigs for this; the generic case now does the same, and
sizes the aperture's L1 entries to fit.

Rebase-risk: low (two expressions in arm_vm_init()).
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
@@ -1849,7 +1849,16 @@
 #endif
 
 #if defined(ARM_LARGE_MEMORY)
+#if GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM && !XNU_MONITOR
+	/*
+	 * Generic Arm: room for the twig-alignment padding arm_vm_physmap_slide()
+	 * puts between the physical aperture's segments (below).
+	 */
+	unsigned long physmap_l1_entries = ((real_phys_size + ARM64_PHYSMAP_SLIDE_RANGE +
+	    (PTOV_TABLE_SIZE * ARM_TT_TWIG_SIZE)) >> ARM_TT_L1_SHIFT) + 1;
+#else
 	unsigned long physmap_l1_entries = ((real_phys_size + ARM64_PHYSMAP_SLIDE_RANGE) >> ARM_TT_L1_SHIFT) + 1;
+#endif
 	physmap_base = VM_MIN_KERNEL_ADDRESS - (physmap_l1_entries << ARM_TT_L1_SHIFT);
 #else
 	physmap_base = phystokv(args->topOfKernelData);
@@ -1875,7 +1884,20 @@
 #if defined(ARM_LARGE_MEMORY)
 	/* For large memory systems with no PPL such as virtual machines */
 	static_memory_end = phystokv(args->topOfKernelData);
+#if GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM
+	/*
+	 * Generic Arm: arm_vm_physmap_init() places the memory above the kernel
+	 * after the memory below it, moved up to keep its offset within a twig
+	 * (block mappings), so the aperture can end up to a twig past
+	 * physmap_base + real_phys_size when the kernel's own region is smaller
+	 * than a twig. Allow for it as the !ARM_LARGE_MEMORY case does, or
+	 * copyio's bounds check (copy_validate_kernel_addr()) rejects the
+	 * aperture addresses of the highest pages.
+	 */
+	physmap_end = physmap_base + real_phys_size + (PTOV_TABLE_SIZE * ARM_TT_TWIG_SIZE);
+#else
 	physmap_end = physmap_base + real_phys_size;
+#endif
 #else
 	static_memory_end = physmap_base + mem_size + (PTOV_TABLE_SIZE * ARM_TT_TWIG_SIZE); // worst possible case for block alignment
 	physmap_end = physmap_base + real_phys_size + (PTOV_TABLE_SIZE * ARM_TT_TWIG_SIZE);
